GitOps CI/CD Pipeline Integration (Jenkins + GitOps)

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m learning how to integrate CI/CD pipelines with GitOps workflows 🚀
This is a real-world DevOps architecture used in many companies.

🔹 CI Pipeline + GitOps Flow
Traditional CI/CD flow:
 Code → Build → Test → Deploy
GitOps-enhanced flow:
 Code → CI Pipeline → Update Git → GitOps Tool → Kubernetes
Here’s how it works:
Developer pushes code
CI tool builds and tests the application
CI updates deployment YAML (image version) in Git
GitOps tool detects change and deploys automatically

🔹 Role of Jenkins
 Jenkins is commonly used as a CI tool.
Builds application
Runs tests
Pushes updated configuration to Git

🔹 Automation Pipeline Design
A complete automated pipeline includes:
Code commit trigger
Build & test stage
Docker image creation
Push image to registry
Update Git repo (YAML)
GitOps deployment

🔹 Why This Approach is Powerful
Fully automated deployment process
Clear separation of CI and CD
Git remains the single source of truth
Easy rollback and tracking

💡 Key Insight:
 CI tools like Jenkins handle building and testing, while GitOps ensures deployment happens automatically from Git, creating a powerful and scalable pipeline.
